# Moddule 08: Describing Linux Process
# Real-World Example

# Real-World Example

Suppose you run the following command:

```bash
firefox
```

Linux creates a Firefox process.

Firefox may create several child processes, such as:

- Rendering Engine
- GPU Process
- Network Process
- Audio Process

Each child process performs a separate task independently.

This enables Linux to efficiently manage multitasking.

---

# Process Creation Flow

```text
User Runs a Command
        │
        ▼
Kernel Creates a Process
        │
        ▼
CPU and Memory Are Allocated
        │
        ▼
Program Executes
        │
        ▼
Child Processes Created (If Required)
        │
        ▼
Execution Completed
        │
        ▼
Resources Released
        │
        ▼
Zombie Entry Removed
```

---

# 📌 Quick Revision

| Term | Description |
|------|-------------|
| Process | Running instance of a program |
| PID | Process ID |
| PPID | Parent Process ID |
| Parent Process | Original process |
| Child Process | Created using `fork()` |
| `fork()` | Creates a child process |
| `exec()` | Replaces the current process image with a new program |
| Zombie | A terminated process that still has an entry in the process table |
| `systemd` | The first Linux process (PID 1) |

---

# 📖 Key Takeaways

- Every running program is a process.
- Every process receives CPU time and memory from the Linux Kernel.
- Every process has a unique Process ID (PID).
- Child processes are created using the `fork()` system call.
- Child processes inherit many properties from their parent process.
- Parent processes often wait while child processes complete execution.
- A completed process may briefly become a Zombie process.
- All Linux processes ultimately originate from **systemd (PID 1)**.

---

# 💡 Remember

> **Think of a Process as an employee in a company.**
>
> - The **Kernel** is the Manager.
> - The **Program** is the Job Description.
> - The **Process** is the Employee performing the work.
> - The **fork()** system call hires a new employee (Child Process).
> - The **exec()** system call assigns a new job to that employee.
> - When the work is complete, the employee exits.
> - Until HR (the Parent Process) finishes the paperwork, the employee briefly appears as a **Zombie Process**.

